/*!
    \page working-with-server.html
    \title Working with a Server
    \previouspage OPC UA Server
    \nextpage Configuration

    This chapter describes the common end-to-end scenarios that combine the client and
    the server halves of the application.

    \section1 Design, run, and test a server locally

    \list 1
        \li In Server Studio, design an address space and set an endpoint and security
            (see \l {OPC UA Server}).
        \li Start the server. The runtime process starts and reports its endpoint.
        \li Use \e{Open in Client} to connect the built-in client to the running server;
            the client connects to the local endpoint anonymously.
        \li Browse the server, read and write variables, and subscribe to values
            (see \l {OPC UA Client}).
    \endlist

    Because the server runs in its own process, you can stop, restart, or kill it while
    the client stays open — a realistic way to test client reconnect behavior.

    \section1 Test security and identities

    Configure the server with signing and encryption and with username/password users,
    then connect the client using the matching security policy and identity. This
    exercises certificate exchange (using each side's PKI store) and access control end
    to end.

    \section1 Simulate live data

    Attach simulations to variables (counter, sine, ramp, and so on) so the server emits
    changing values. Subscribe to those variables in the client and watch the
    \e{Data Access} view and trend panel update from real data-change notifications.

    \section1 Clone an existing server

    Connect the client to a third-party server, browse the part of the address space you
    care about, and clone it into a new server project. You then have an editable
    \c .uaserver reproduction you can run locally, secure, and simulate.

    Variables keep the data type of the source server. Vendor type aliases, such as the
    CODESYS IEC types STRING or UINT, are resolved through the server's type hierarchy to
    their OPC UA base type (String, UInt16, ...). Date/time values and types that cannot
    be resolved are created as String variables; the status message after the clone
    states how many variables use such a substitute type.

    Structures keep their structure type. Its name is the name of the DataType node on
    the server (for CODESYS, for example \c ST_HMI_Button); a string member called
    \c structType is used only when the server does not name the type. The structure
    variable has no value of its own: its members, and the elements of an array of
    structures, are its child nodes, exactly as CODESYS publishes them. Arrays keep their
    element type, rank and dimensions, so an array of structures, an array of a simple
    type and a multi-dimensional array (for example \c{Int32[2,3]}) are reproduced as such.
    The type column of the server tree shows these types, for example
    \c{ST_HMI_Button[13]}.

    The clone runs with a progress dialog; \e{Cancel} stops it and leaves the current
    server project unchanged. The browse is bounded by the node limit chosen in the clone
    dialog (50 000, 200 000 or 1 000 000 nodes; 200 000 by default), a depth of 64
    levels, 10 minutes in total and 30 seconds without a server response. When a limit
    ends the clone, or some nodes cannot be read or browsed, the status message says so
    and the clone is incomplete.

    \section1 Exchange address spaces

    Use NodeSet2 import/export to move an address space between this tool and other OPC
    UA tooling, or to keep a versioned copy of a designed model.

    \section1 Emulate PLC request/response handshakes

    Add behavior rules to a designed server so that a client write triggers a server-side
    response — for example \c PageRequest → \c PageResponse (see \l {OPC UA Server}). This
    emulates the handshakes an HMI expects from a PLC, letting you exercise an HMI or client
    against realistic request/response behavior that a static address space cannot provide.
*/
